<http://purl.org/edu/bloom#CognitiveProcessLevel> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel
<http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#CS2023SkillLevel
<http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#CS2023SkillLevel> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Event
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Event> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Quality
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Quality> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#QualityValue
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#QualityValue> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Situation
<http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Situation> rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AcquisitionContext
ontoksd:AcquisitionContext rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Context ;
                           rdfs:comment """AcquisitionContext

A specialized contextual setting in which teaching, learning, training, or assessment activities take place and within which competence acquisition, development, or demonstration can be facilitated, observed, or evaluated.

In OntoKSD, AcquisitionContext represents the pedagogically relevant situation that frames the realization of instructional and evaluative processes oriented toward competency development. It captures the instructional, environmental, organizational, and temporal conditions under which learners engage in activities designed to acquire, improve, or demonstrate competencies.

An AcquisitionContext may encompass elements such as the learning environment, available resources, institutional or task-related constraints, pedagogical strategies, collaboration arrangements, and assessment conditions, insofar as these factors jointly shape the conditions under which competence-oriented learning processes occur.

Unlike the more general Context, which may refer to any situation in which a competence is applicable, manifested, observed, or evidenced, AcquisitionContext is specifically restricted to contexts associated with educational, training, or formative-evaluative processes. Its role is to situate instructional entities and assessment occurrences within a concrete pedagogical setting, thereby providing the background against which evidence of competence acquisition or development may be produced and interpreted.

Typical examples of AcquisitionContext include:

a laboratory session in a computer science course in which students collaboratively design and test algorithms;

an online simulation environment used to train project management skills under time-constrained conditions;

a capstone project developed in an engineering program with interdisciplinary teamwork and industry mentorship.

Ontologically, AcquisitionContext is modeled as a subclass of Context and, therefore, as a gufo:Situation, since it denotes a temporally bounded situational configuration that frames the execution of instructional units and related assessment events in competency-oriented educational settings.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationConstraint
ontoksd:ActivationConstraint rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                             rdfs:comment """ActivationConstraint captures the conditions, limitations, or requirements that delimit how a competence is expected to be exercised within a specific instructional context, including constraints related to tools, representations, time, collaboration, or evaluation conditions.

Ontological Note:

ActivationConstraint constrains the conditions under which a competence may be activated, demonstrated, or assessed, without altering the conceptual definition of the competence itself.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationFunction
ontoksd:ActivationFunction rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Quality> ;
                           rdfs:comment """ActivationFunction
A controlled classificatory concept used to indicate the pedagogical function that a competence assumes in a specific instance of CompetenceActivation. Rather than defining different ontological kinds of activation relators, ActivationFunction qualifies how a given Competence participates in the relation mediated by CompetenceActivation between an InstructionalEntity and a Competence.

This class is intended to capture distinctions such as whether a competence is activated as a target of learning and development or as a prerequisite required for engagement in an instructional context. These distinctions do not denote different types of relators, but different functional roles played by the same general activation relation in a pedagogical setting.

Instances of ActivationFunction should therefore be used as controlled values associated with CompetenceActivation through a property such as hasActivationFunction.

Examples of instances:

targetFunction: the competence is intended to be developed, mobilized, or demonstrated as an instructional target.

prerequisiteFunction: the competence is presupposed as a prior requirement for participation or performance in the instructional entity.

Ontological rationale:
ActivationFunction is modeled as a qualifier of CompetenceActivation in order to preserve the ontological unity of the relator while explicitly representing pedagogical variation in how competencies are positioned within instructional contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationMode
ontoksd:ActivationMode rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """ActivationMode represents the manner in which a competence is activated within a specific instructional context. It characterizes the cognitive or operational mode through which the competence is mobilized (e.g., constructive, analytical, justificatory), as determined by task design and expected learner actions.

ActivationMode is not necessarily exclusive; a competence may be activated through multiple modes within the same instructional entity.

Ontological Note:

ActivationMode does not redefine the competence itself; it qualifies how the competence is exercised in a particular competence–instructional entity relation.""" ;
                       skos:example """Example Set 1 — Same Competence, Different ActivationModes
Competence: C01 — Develop problem solutions using Automata


1. ActivationMode: Constructive

Instructional context: Learners are required to design and implement a finite automaton from a problem description.

Observable actions:

- Define states and transitions

- Construct the automaton formally

- Ensure acceptance of the target language

Why this is a different mode: The competence is activated through creation and synthesis of an artifact.


2. ActivationMode: Analytical

Instructional context: Learners receive a pre-defined automaton and must analyze its behavior.

Observable actions:

- Trace execution paths

- Identify incorrect transitions

- Explain acceptance or rejection of strings

Key point: The learner does not build the automaton; they reason about an existing one.

➡️ Same competence, different activation mode.


3. ActivationMode: Diagnostic (or Debugging)

Instructional context: Learners are given a faulty automaton and must locate and correct errors.

Observable actions:

- Detect inconsistencies

- Propose minimal fixes

- Justify corrections

Why it matters: This mode emphasizes problem diagnosis, not construction.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 2 — ActivationMode vs. Bloom Level (Important Distinction)
Competence: C08 — Identify Turing Machine variants

1. ActivationMode: Descriptive

Task: “List and describe different variants of Turing Machines.”

- Bloom level: Understand

- Mode: descriptive recognition


2. ActivationMode: Evaluative

Task: “Assess which Turing Machine variant is most appropriate for a given system.”

- Bloom level: Analyze

- Mode: comparative evaluation under constraints


Insight: Bloom’s level changes, but ActivationMode captures the nature of engagement, not the taxonomy itself.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 3 — Specialization Triggered by ActivationMode Change
C07 vs. C07.01 (from CSP Phase 3)

C07 — Develop solutions using Turing Machines

- Typical ActivationMode: Constructive

- Context: single-agent, isolated model


C07.01 — Develop integrated multi-agent solutions using Turing Machines

ActivationModes:

- Constructive (building each TM)

- Integrative (coordinating multiple TMs)

- Analytical (validating system-wide behavior)

Why specialization was needed: The set of activation modes expanded and became structurally different, signaling contextual scope expansion, not just reuse.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 4 — Same Task, Multiple ActivationModes
Task: “The Farmer Robot and the Feeder Robot”

A single task may activate the same competence in multiple modes simultaneously.

Competence: C09 — Apply Turing Machine variants
ActivationMode   x   Manifestation
Applied	      x   Selecting a TM variant for a robot
Analytical	      x   Justifying the choice under system constraints
Coordinative	      x    Aligning variants across agents

ActivationMode is not mutually exclusive: It reflects how the competence is mobilized, not a single step.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationQualifier
ontoksd:ActivationQualifier rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Quality> ;
                            rdfs:comment """Definition:

ActivationQualifier represents a contextual descriptor that qualifies the manner in which a competence is expected to be activated, demonstrated, or assessed within a specific instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, it groups the main categories of contextual qualification—such as constraints, modes, and roles—used to specify how, why, and under which conditions a competence is expected to be addressed in a given instructional context.

Ontological Note:

ActivationQualifier constrains or characterizes the activation of a competence in context without modifying the conceptual definition of the competence itself.
It is modeled as an abstract superclass to ensure consistent and extensible representation of contextual competence activation metadata.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationRole
ontoksd:ActivationRole rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """ActivationRole specifies the instructional function assumed by a competence within a given instructional entity, such as primary learning objective, supporting competence, or implicit prerequisite.

ActivationRole describes why a competence is mobilized in a given instructional entity, i.e., the instructional function it plays in that context.

Ontological Note:

The same competence may assume different activation roles across instructional entities; therefore, ActivationRole is modeled as a contextual attribute of competence activation rather than an intrinsic property of the competence.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessment
ontoksd:Assessment rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalUnit ;
                   rdfs:comment """Assessment
A subclass of InstructionalUnit representing a pedagogically defined evaluative unit whose primary purpose is to judge, verify, or interpret the demonstration of competencies in an educational context.

In OntoKSD, Assessment denotes an evaluative instructional unit such as an exam, quiz, practical test, rubric-based evaluation, oral presentation assessment, project evaluation, or other structured form of pedagogical evaluation. Its primary function is not simply to support learner engagement, but to provide a designed basis for evaluative judgment regarding competency demonstration.

Assessment is modeled at the level of pedagogical specification rather than as the concrete event in which evaluation is actually carried out. The concrete realization of an assessment is represented separately by AssessmentOccurrence, which captures the eventive occurrence of that evaluative unit in a specific situation.

An Assessment may therefore:

be associated with one or more competencies through competence-related instructional relations;

be based on one or more learning activities;

use one or more educational resources;

be realized through one or more assessment occurrences;

be semantically classified through controlled assessment-related concepts.

Typical examples of Assessment include:

a written exam on formal languages;

a practical programming test;

a rubric-based evaluation of a software design report;

an oral assessment on ethical issues in computing;

a project presentation assessment;

a simulation-based evaluation of problem-solving performance.

Ontological rationale:
Assessment is modeled as a subclass of InstructionalUnit because it represents a bounded pedagogical unit whose primary function is evaluative. It is distinct from LearningActivity, whose primary purpose is learning support and engagement, and from AssessmentOccurrence, which represents the concrete realization of that evaluative unit in a specific event. This separation allows OntoKSD to distinguish evaluative specification from evaluative occurrence while preserving their semantic relation.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentOccurrence
ontoksd:AssessmentOccurrence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Event> ,
                                             [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:involvesAssessmentParticipation ;
                                                                      owl:minQualifiedCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation
                                                                    ]
                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:realizes ;
                                                                      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:Assessment
                                                                    ]
                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:usesProficiencyScheme ;
                                                                      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyScheme
                                                                    ]
                                                                  ) ;
                                               rdf:type owl:Class
                                             ] ;
                             rdfs:comment """AssessmentOccurrence
A concrete evaluative event in which an Assessment is carried out in a specific educational situation.

In OntoKSD, AssessmentOccurrence represents the eventive realization of an Assessment. While Assessment denotes the evaluative instructional unit at the level of pedagogical specification, AssessmentOccurrence captures the concrete evaluative event corresponding to that unit, situated in specific contextual conditions and involving specific participants.

An AssessmentOccurrence may therefore:

realize a previously defined Assessment;

occur within a specific acquisition context;

involve one or more assessment participations;

produce evidence in the context of that concrete evaluative event;

function as the common evaluative event from which participant-specific results and judgments may later be derived.

AssessmentOccurrence is modeled as a gufo:Event because it represents something that happens in time and in context, rather than a standing evaluative specification. This distinction allows OntoKSD to separate evaluative design from evaluative execution while preserving their semantic connection.

In OntoKSD, the evidence associated with an AssessmentOccurrence is understood as evidence produced in the context of that concrete evaluative event. In group-based assessments, such evidence may be collective, even when resulting scores or competency judgments are later assigned individually to participants. For this reason, AssessmentOccurrence functions as the contextual anchor for shared evaluative production, while participant-specific evaluative relations are represented separately through AssessmentParticipation.

Typical examples of AssessmentOccurrence include:

the realization of a written exam on formal languages on a given date;

the concrete execution of a programming test in a lab session;

the presentation and evaluation of a group solution in a task-based assessment setting;

the ocurrence of a rubric-based oral assessment in a classroom context;

the collective completion of a project-based assessment in a specific evaluation session.

Ontological rationale:
AssessmentOccurrence is introduced to represent the concrete occurrence of evaluation, as distinct from the more abstract evaluative structure represented by Assessment. This separation supports the modeling of contextualized evidence, participant involvement, and the distinction between collective evaluative events and participant-specific assessment results.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentParticipation
ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                                [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasLearner ;
                                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:Learner
                                                                       ]
                                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:occursInAssessmentOccurrence ;
                                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:AssessmentOccurrence
                                                                       ]
                                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasScore ;
                                                                         owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                         owl:onDataRange xsd:double
                                                                       ]
                                                                     ) ;
                                                  rdf:type owl:Class
                                                ] ;
                                rdfs:comment """AssessmentParticipation
A relator that represents the participant-specific evaluative relation established between a Learner and an AssessmentOccurrence, optionally also involving an Assessor.

In OntoKSD, AssessmentParticipation is used to mediate the individualized evaluative relation established within a concrete assessment event. It connects the learner whose performance is being evaluated to a specific AssessmentOccurrence and, where applicable, also connects the assessor responsible for judgment, review, or grading in that same occurrence.

This class is particularly important in assessment settings where:

multiple learners participate in the same assessment occurrence;

evidence is produced collectively at the occurrence level;

evaluative outcomes must still be attributed individually to participants; or

the same collective result is assigned to several learners while remaining individually recordable.

An AssessmentParticipation may therefore:

link a learner to a specific assessment occurrence;

link an assessor to that same occurrence, where applicable;

serve as the participant-specific anchor for evaluative results, scores, and competency-related judgments derived from the occurrence.

AssessmentParticipation is modeled as a gufo:Relator because it does not merely denote the presence of participants in an assessment event, but the contextualized evaluative relation that holds among them in that event. Its identity depends on the specific occurrence and the participants it mediates.

In OntoKSD, collective evidence may be associated with the AssessmentOccurrence as a whole, while AssessmentParticipation captures the individualized evaluative interpretation of that event for each learner. For this reason, participant-specific scores and related judgments should be modeled at the level of AssessmentParticipation rather than directly in AssessmentOccurrence.

Typical examples of AssessmentParticipation include:

the participation of a specific learner in a written exam occurrence, with an individual score assigned;

the participation of each member of a student group in a shared project assessment occurrence, each one receiving the same group score;

the participation of a learner in a group-based assessment occurrence, but with an individualized final score assigned by the assessor;

the evaluative relation between a learner, an assessor, and a concrete oral presentation occurrence.

Ontological rationale:
AssessmentParticipation is introduced to distinguish the collective evaluative event represented by AssessmentOccurrence from the participant-specific evaluative relation established within that event. This separation allows OntoKSD to represent group-based assessments, shared evidence, individualized grading, and participant-level competency judgments in a semantically precise way.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessor
ontoksd:Assessor rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Attitude
ontoksd:Attitude rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Disposition ;
                 rdfs:comment """Attitude
A subclass of Disposition representing dispositional elements that are primarily attitudinal in nature. In OntoKSD, this class is included to support interoperability with KSA-oriented models and literature in which attitudes are explicitly treated as a major component of competence."""@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Attitude"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                             owl:unionOf ( ontoksd:BNCCGeneralCompetence
                                                           ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence
                                                         )
                                           ] ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ExternalCompetence ;
                       rdfs:comment """Represents a competence defined in the Base Nacional Comum Curricular (BNCC), the official Brazilian national curriculum framework.

BNCCCompetence models competencies at a normative and policy-oriented level, expressing broad educational expectations established by national regulation. These competencies are not operationalized within OntoKSD, but may be aligned with one or more OntoKSD competencies to demonstrate curricular coherence, regulatory compliance, or pedagogical coverage.

BNCCCompetence provides a semantic anchor that enables OntoKSD to relate instructional-level competencies to national curriculum standards, without asserting semantic equivalence or direct substitution.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCGeneralCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCGeneralCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                              owl:disjointWith ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence ;
                              rdfs:comment """Represents a General Competence defined by the BNCC, which expresses broad, transversal educational objectives applicable across multiple knowledge domains, disciplines, and educational stages.

BNCCGeneralCompetence captures high-level dispositions, cognitive attitudes, and overarching capabilities (e.g., critical thinking, communication, ethical responsibility) that are intentionally cross-cutting and non-disciplinary in nature.

Within OntoKSD, BNCCGeneralCompetence instances are used exclusively as external alignment references, allowing multiple domain-specific competencies to be mapped to shared national educational goals, without imposing additional structural or cognitive constraints on the internal competency model.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCSpecificCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                               rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                               rdfs:comment """Represents a Specific Competence defined by the BNCC, typically associated with a particular area of knowledge, discipline, or curricular component.

BNCCSpecificCompetence expresses domain-oriented educational expectations at a normative level, serving as a reference point for curriculum design and evaluation. These competencies are more contextualized than general competencies, but still remain non-operational within OntoKSD.

In OntoKSD, BNCCSpecificCompetence is used to establish semantic alignment between externally defined curricular requirements and internally specified competencies, supporting traceability between instructional outcomes and national curriculum mandates.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Competence
ontoksd:Competence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                     owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasCompetenceConstituent ;
                                     owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                     owl:onClass ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent
                                   ] ;
                   owl:disjointWith ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ,
                                    ontoksd:Evidence ,
                                    ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence ,
                                    ontoksd:Profile ;
                   rdfs:comment """A formal competency specification that represents an integrated and reusable set of knowledge, skills, and dispositions expected for competent performance in a given domain.

In OntoKSD, Competence is modeled as a conceptual reference entity used to specify what is expected for successful performance, independently of any particular learner, assessment event, or evidential record. It functions as a stable semantic anchor for curriculum design, instructional alignment, competency specification, and competency-oriented evaluation.

A Competence is constituted by one or more competence constituents, such as knowledge, skills, and dispositions, which together define its internal semantic structure. In this sense, Competence does not represent an observed performance or an individual achievement, but rather the specification against which such performances may later be interpreted.

This distinction allows OntoKSD to separate:

the competency specification itself, represented by Competence;

its constitutive elements, represented by CompetenceConstituent and its subclasses; and

its contextualized evidential records, represented by EvidencedCompetence.

Ontological rationale:
Competence is modeled as a reusable specification entity because OntoKSD needs to represent competencies as stable curricular and semantic references that may be activated in instructional contexts, related to external frameworks, and later evidenced by specific individuals in specific situations."""@en ;
                   rdfs:label "Competence"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceConstituent
ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                    owl:unionOf ( ontoksd:Disposition
                                                                  ontoksd:Knowledge
                                                                  ontoksd:Skill
                                                                )
                                                  ] ;
                              owl:disjointWith ontoksd:Evidence ,
                                               ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent ,
                                               ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill ;
                              dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Cited as Competency in Miranda, S. et al. (2018). An Ontology-Based Model for Competence Management. Journal of Intelligent Manufacturing."@en ;
                              rdfs:comment """An abstract constituent of a Competence that represents one of the semantically distinct kinds of elements through which a competence is specified in OntoKSD.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceConstituent is introduced as a unifying superclass for Knowledge, Skill, and Disposition in order to represent their shared structural role as constitutive elements of a competence. Its purpose is not to erase the ontological differences among these subclasses, but to provide a controlled abstraction for cases in which they must be handled uniformly within competency specification.

Accordingly, CompetenceConstituent should be understood as a structural and functional abstraction, rather than as a claim that knowledge, skills, and dispositions are ontologically identical. These subclasses remain semantically distinct:

Knowledge represents conceptual or content-oriented elements;

Skill represents capability-oriented elements related to competent performance;

Disposition represents inclination-oriented, attitudinal, belief-based, or value-based elements that shape competent action.

The role of CompetenceConstituent is therefore to capture what these subclasses have in common only insofar as they can all function as constitutive elements of a competence. This abstraction supports concise modeling, reuse of generalized relations, and the representation of evidence, profiling, contextualization, and proficiency attribution at the element level, while preserving the semantic specificity of each subclass.

CompetenceConstituent is especially useful when OntoKSD needs to refer generically to any element that composes a competence, for example through relations such as hasCompetenceConstituent or in contextualized evidential records such as EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

Ontological clarification:
CompetenceConstituent is not intended as a homogeneous ontological category in a strong metaphysical sense. Rather, it is a controlled abstraction introduced to support the formal specification of competencies and the uniform treatment of their constitutive elements in competency-oriented educational modeling.\"\"\"@en .""" ;
                              rdfs:label "CompetenceConstituent"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceRelator
ontoksd:CompetenceRelator rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                            owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCompetence ;
                                            owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                            owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                          ] ,
                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                            owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesInstructionalUnit ;
                                            owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                            owl:onClass ontoksd:InstructionalUnit
                                          ] ;
                          rdfs:comment "Reifies the relationship between a instructional entity and a competence, with support for annotations."@en ;
                          rdfs:label "CompetenceActivation" ;
                          skos:definition "A reified relation that associates an instructional entity with a competence, specifying whether it is targeted or required as a prerequisite."@en ;
                          skos:scopeNote "Allows the same instructional entity to be linked to multiple competencies with distinct roles (e.g., prerequisite vs. learning outcome)."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceScope
ontoksd:CompetenceScope rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:comment """CompetenceScope
A classificatory category used to indicate the scope or applicability of a Competence within educational and professional contexts. It distinguishes whether a competence is specific to the Computing domain, foundational for broader learning and development, professional in orientation, or transversal across domains and contexts.

CompetenceScope is intended to support the semantic classification of competencies according to their breadth of applicability and pedagogical or professional orientation. This classification helps differentiate competencies that are domain-specific from those that are broadly transferable or oriented toward professional practice.

Instances of CompetenceScope should be used as controlled values associated with Competence through a property such as hasCompetenceScope.

Instance: computingSpecific

computingSpecific
A scope classification indicating that a competence is specific to the Computing domain. Competencies with this scope are directly related to computing concepts, methods, techniques, practices, or problem-solving activities, and are not primarily intended as general-purpose or cross-domain competencies.

Examples include competencies related to programming, algorithms, data structures, software design, computer systems, artificial intelligence, or other core areas of Computing.

Instance: foundational

foundational
A scope classification indicating that a competence provides essential grounding for learning, reasoning, or performance and serves as a basis for the development of other competencies. Foundational competencies are not necessarily exclusive to Computing, but they are often required to support more specific or advanced forms of competence.

Examples may include logical reasoning, mathematical thinking, abstraction, problem interpretation, or other competencies that function as conceptual or cognitive foundations for further learning and practice.

Instance: professional

professional
A scope classification indicating that a competence is oriented toward professional performance, workplace practice, or the exercise of responsibilities in occupational settings. Competencies with this scope are associated with professional conduct, collaboration, project execution, communication, ethics, or other capabilities relevant to professional activity.

This scope may include competencies that are valued in Computing professions but are not limited to domain-specific technical knowledge alone.

Instance: transversal

transversal
A scope classification indicating that a competence is transferable across multiple domains, disciplines, or contexts. Competencies with this scope are not restricted to a single knowledge area or professional field and may be mobilized in varied educational, social, or occupational situations.

Examples include communication, teamwork, autonomy, critical thinking, adaptability, and other broadly applicable competencies that can support performance across different contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Context
ontoksd:Context rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Situation> ;
                rdfs:comment """Context

A situational setting that frames the applicability, manifestation, observation, or evidencing of a competence or one of its constituent elements.

In OntoKSD, Context represents the set of relevant circumstances under which a competence, competence constituent, or related evidence becomes meaningful, observable, or applicable. It captures the situational conditions that delimit how competence is expressed, recognized, or interpreted in a given scenario.

A Context may include environmental, organizational, social, task-related, or domain-specific conditions that shape the activation, demonstration, or evaluation of competence. Typical examples include a workplace setting, a collaborative problem-solving situation, a laboratory activity, or another concrete situation in which competence is applied, observed, or evidenced.

Context is intentionally modeled as a general class. It does not refer exclusively to instructional or learning situations, but to any relevant situational configuration that frames competence-related phenomena. More specific contextual forms, such as those directly associated with teaching, learning, training, or assessment processes, should be represented through specialized subclasses such as AcquisitionContext.

Ontologically, Context is modeled as a gufo:Situation, since it denotes a temporally and circumstantially bounded configuration that provides the background against which competence-related manifestations can occur, be observed, or be interpreted.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Course
ontoksd:Course rdf:type owl:Class ;
               rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind> ;
               rdfs:comment """Course
A curricular aggregating unit that represents a taught disciplinary component within which instructional units are organized and competencies are specified at an aggregated curricular level.

In OntoKSD, Course is modeled as a curricular unit distinct from InstructionalUnit, rather than as a subtype of it. Its purpose is to represent the disciplinary structure within which multiple instructional units—such as learning activities, learning tasks, and assessments—may be organized, coordinated, and pedagogically articulated as part of a broader curricular component.

A Course does not itself represent the concrete instructional situations in which competencies are practiced, supported, or assessed. Instead, it provides the curricular framework within which such instructional units are situated. In this sense, Course supports curricular aggregation and alignment, whereas InstructionalUnit supports the representation of bounded pedagogical units at the instructional level.

A Course may therefore:

aggregate one or more instructional units through relations such as hasInstructionalUnit;

be associated with one or more competencies through mediated curricular specifications such as CourseCompetenceSpecification;

be identified and described through properties such as title, identifier, description, and syllabus text.

At the current scope of OntoKSD, Course is intentionally modeled without temporal specificity, since it represents a curricular component as defined in the educational structure, rather than a particular course offering in a given semester, class group, or academic period.

Ontological rationale:
Course is introduced to distinguish the curricular organization of teaching from the instructional units through which competencies are pedagogically addressed in more specific contexts. This separation preserves the distinction between curricular aggregation and instructional structuring, while allowing OntoKSD to represent both levels in an integrated and semantically coherent manner.""" ,
                            "Course is best modeled as a Kind in gUFO because it represents a curricular unit with its own identity criteria in the educational domain, rather than a mere classificatory type. Instances of Course correspond to identifiable disciplinary units that aggregate instructional entities and address competencies at the curricular level." .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CourseCompetenceSpecification
ontoksd:CourseCompetenceSpecification rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                      rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                                      [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCourse ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Course
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCourseCompetence ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:requiresMinimumProficiencyLevel ;
                                                                               owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:isMandatory ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                                                             ]
                                                                           ) ;
                                                        rdf:type owl:Class
                                                      ] ;
                                      rdfs:comment """CourseCompetenceSpecification
A relator that mediates the curricular association between a Course and a Competence, enabling this association to be explicitly specified and semantically qualified.

In OntoKSD, CourseCompetenceSpecification is used to represent the fact that a given competence is included, expected, or required within the curricular scope of a course. Rather than linking Course directly to Competence through a simple object property, the ontology introduces this relator in order to capture the qualified nature of that association.

This modeling choice allows OntoKSD to represent not only that a competence is related to a course, but also how that relation is defined from a curricular perspective. For example, a CourseCompetenceSpecification may indicate whether the competence is mandatory within the course and whether a minimum proficiency level is expected.

A CourseCompetenceSpecification may therefore:

mediate the relation between a Course and a Competence;

indicate whether the competence is mandatory within the curricular specification of the course;

specify a minimum required proficiency level associated with that competence in the context of the course.

This class does not represent the actual activation, practice, or assessment of the competence in a concrete instructional situation. Such aspects remain associated with instructional units and assessment occurrences. Instead, CourseCompetenceSpecification represents the curricular-level specification that a course includes or requires a given competence.

For example, a CourseCompetenceSpecification may indicate whether the competence is mandatory within the course and which minimum proficiency level the course is expected to address so that the competence can be considered meaningfully contemplated in its curricular scope.

Ontological rationale:
CourseCompetenceSpecification is modeled as a relator because the association between a course and a competence is not merely extensional, but qualified and semantically meaningful. Reifying this association allows OntoKSD to represent curricular constraints, requirements, and expectations that would be lost in a simple direct relation between Course and Competence.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Disposition
ontoksd:Disposition rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ;
                    rdfs:comment """Disposition
A competence element representing relatively stable inclination-oriented aspects that influence how a person tends to think, judge, value, or act in relevant situations. In OntoKSD, Disposition is used as a broad category for representing dispositional dimensions of competence, in alignment with competency models that include dispositions alongside knowledge and skills.

This class is intended to support the representation of attitudes, beliefs, values, and other related orientation-based elements that shape competent performance across educational and professional contexts. Disposition therefore provides a semantically inclusive category that can accommodate both KSD-oriented and KSA-oriented interpretations of competence models.

Instances or subclasses of Disposition may be further classified through DispositionCategory in order to indicate the predominant dispositional nature of the element."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#DispositionCategory
ontoksd:DispositionCategory rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """DispositionCategory
A classificatory category used to indicate the predominant dispositional nature of a Disposition element within a competency specification. In OntoKSD, this class supports the semantic classification of dispositions according to whether they are primarily attitudinal, belief-based, or value-based.

This classification is intended to provide a lightweight and semantically coherent mechanism for organizing dispositional elements without overcomplicating the ontology. It helps distinguish different kinds of dispositional orientation while preserving the broader unifying role of the class Disposition.

Instances of DispositionCategory should be used as controlled values associated with Disposition through a property such as hasDispositionCategory.""" ,
                                         """Instance: attitudeCategory

attitudeCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly attitudinal in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express evaluative or affective orientations toward objects, situations, practices, people, or forms of action.

Examples may include openness to collaboration, willingness to engage, persistence toward challenges, or responsibility toward task completion when these are modeled primarily as attitudinal tendencies.

Instance: beliefCategory

beliefCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly belief-based in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express convictions, assumptions, or internalized understandings that shape how a person interprets situations and approaches action.

Examples may include beliefs about the value of systematic reasoning, the importance of ethical responsibility, or the relevance of collaboration in professional practice.

Instance: valueCategory

valueCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly value-based in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express normative commitments, principles, or value orientations that guide judgment and behavior.

Examples may include valuing inclusiveness, valuing professional integrity, valuing accuracy, or valuing social responsibility in the design and use of computing artefacts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Document
ontoksd:Document rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:comment """A documentary artefact that records, contains, organizes, or supports evidence related to one or more evidenced competencies.

In OntoKSD, Document represents a material or digital record that may serve as documentary support for evidence. Examples may include certificates, reports, transcripts, portfolios, declarations, or other records that document competence-related achievements or performances.

A Document may aggregate one or more instances of Evidence and may serve as a supporting artefact for the identification of evidenced competencies or competence constituents.

Document is intentionally modeled as a general documentary class, without committing to finer distinctions such as validation records, certificates, or internal document sections, unless such distinctions are needed elsewhere in the ontology.""" ;
                 rdfs:label "Document"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EducationalResource
ontoksd:EducationalResource rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """EducationalResource
A pedagogical artefact used to support, mediate, document, or facilitate teaching, learning, or assessment processes within an instructional context.

In OntoKSD, EducationalResource is modeled as an entity distinct from InstructionalUnit. It does not represent an instructional action, plan, or event in itself, but rather an artefact that may be used within instructional contexts. This decision avoids conflating instructional units, which are the primary loci of competence activation, with the informational or material artefacts that support them.

Examples of educational resources may include textbooks, articles, slide decks, videos, worksheets, datasets, simulators, source code, rubrics, manuals, or other pedagogically relevant materials.

An EducationalResource may therefore be associated with instructional units through relations such as usesResource, indicating that the resource is employed within a learning activity, learning task, assessment, or other instructional unit.

At the current scope of OntoKSD, EducationalResource is intentionally modeled in a lightweight manner. Its minimal descriptive structure includes properties such as title and description, while its semantic classification may be expressed through controlled concepts, such as SKOS concepts associated with resource-type schemes.

Ontological rationale:
EducationalResource is modeled as a pedagogical artefact rather than as an instructional unit in order to preserve the distinction between pedagogical action and the artefacts used to support it. Instructional units remain the contexts in which competencies are activated, while educational resources function as supporting artefacts within those contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Evidence
ontoksd:Evidence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 owl:disjointWith ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence ,
                                  ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent ,
                                  ontoksd:Profile ;
                 rdfs:comment """A record, artefact, or observable element that supports the identification of a competence or competence constituent as evidenced in a given context.

In OntoKSD, Evidence represents any element used to indicate that a competence or one of its constituent elements has been demonstrated, manifested, or otherwise made observable. It functions as a bridge between competence specifications and their concrete evidencing in educational, professional, or other relevant situations.

An Evidence may be associated with a specific EvidenceType, may be linked to a supporting Document, and may support one or more instances of EvidencedCompetence or EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

Evidence is intentionally modeled in a lightweight manner. Its role is not to represent the full validation, interpretation, or certification process of performance, but simply to denote an element that supports the recognition that a competence has been evidenced.""" ;
                 rdfs:label "Evidence"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidenceType
ontoksd:EvidenceType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment """A semantic classification that characterizes the kind or form of an evidence.

In OntoKSD, EvidenceType is used to distinguish different kinds of evidence according to the form in which they are produced, presented, or recognized. It supports the organization and interpretation of heterogeneous evidence used to indicate that competencies or competence constituents have been evidenced.

Typical instances of EvidenceType may include forms such as written exam, presentation, portfolio evidence, self-assessment, peer assessment, teacher assessment, performance observation, or technical documentation.

EvidenceType does not determine whether an evidence is valid, sufficient, or reliable; it only classifies the kind of evidence involved.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetence
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasEvidencedConstituent ;
                                              owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:supportedByEvidence ;
                                              owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Evidence
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:isEvidencedCompetenceOf ;
                                              owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Profile
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:refersToCompetence ;
                                              owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProficiencyLevel ;
                                              owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                            ] ;
                            rdfs:comment """A contextualized evidential record that represents a specific individual's demonstrated competence in relation to a defined Competence.

In OntoKSD, EvidencedCompetence is used to represent that a competence has been evidenced for a particular learner or profile in a specific evaluative or evidential context. It is not the competence specification itself, but an individual-specific record grounded in concrete evidence, participation, and contextual interpretation.

EvidencedCompetence allows OntoKSD to distinguish between:

the abstract competency specification, represented by Competence; and

the contextualized record that this competence has been demonstrated or evidenced by a particular individual, represented by EvidencedCompetence.

An EvidencedCompetence may therefore:

refer to a specific Competence;

be supported by one or more instances of Evidence;

be derived from an AssessmentParticipation;

be associated with a ProficiencyLevel;

be linked to finer-grained evidential records of its constituents through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

Unlike Competence, which is a reusable conceptual specification, EvidencedCompetence is context-dependent and individual-specific. Its purpose is to support the representation of demonstrated competence, competency profiles, evidence-based interpretation, and proficiency attribution in concrete educational or professional situations.

Ontological rationale:
EvidencedCompetence is introduced to represent the evidential and contextualized side of competency modeling. This separation allows OntoKSD to preserve Competence as a stable specification entity while representing the fact that the same competence may be evidenced differently by different individuals, in different contexts, and with different levels of proficiency."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetenceConstituent
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                       rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:supportedByEvidence ;
                                                         owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:Evidence
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:isEvidencedCompetenceConstituentOf ;
                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:refersToCompetenceConstituent ;
                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProficiencyLevel ;
                                                         owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                       ] ;
                                       rdfs:comment """A contextualized evidential record that represents a specific individual's demonstrated competence constituent in relation to a defined CompetenceConstituent.

In OntoKSD, EvidencedCompetenceConstituent is used to represent that a particular constituent of a competence—such as a Knowledge, Skill, or Disposition element—has been evidenced for a specific individual in a concrete context. It provides a finer-grained evidential layer than EvidencedCompetence, allowing constituent-level representation of demonstrated competency elements.

EvidencedCompetenceConstituent allows OntoKSD to distinguish between:

the abstract constituent of a competency specification, represented by CompetenceConstituent; and

the contextualized record that this constituent has been evidenced for a particular individual, represented by EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

An EvidencedCompetenceConstituent may therefore:

refer to a specific CompetenceConstituent;

be supported by one or more instances of Evidence;

be derived from an AssessmentParticipation;

be associated with a ProficiencyLevel;

be linked to an EvidencedCompetence of which it forms part.

This class is especially useful when evaluation, profiling, or feedback must be expressed at a more detailed level than the competence as a whole, such as when a learner demonstrates some knowledge or skill components of a competence more strongly than others.

Ontological rationale:
EvidencedCompetenceConstituent is introduced to support constituent-level evidential representation. This allows OntoKSD to model fine-grained demonstrated competency structures while preserving the distinction between abstract specification elements and their contextualized evidential records.\"\"\"@en ."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ExternalCompetence
ontoksd:ExternalCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:comment """Represents a competence defined in an external reference framework, normative document, or institutional guideline, which is not authored within the OntoKSD competency specification process.

ExternalCompetence is used to reference, contextualize, and align OntoKSD competencies with competencies defined by authoritative external sources (e.g., national curricula, international frameworks, or professional standards), while preserving the conceptual autonomy of the internal competency model.

Instances of ExternalCompetence do not define learning outcomes directly within OntoKSD. Instead, they serve as alignment targets, supporting curriculum mapping, compliance analysis, and interoperability with external educational frameworks.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#InstructionalUnit
ontoksd:InstructionalUnit rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:comment """InstructionalUnit
A bounded pedagogical unit through which competencies may be activated, practiced, supported, or assessed within an educational context.

In OntoKSD, InstructionalUnit is used as a general class for operational instructional structures such as LearningActivity, LearningTask, and Assessment. It represents a delimited unit of instructional organization at the level of pedagogical specification and instructional structuring.

InstructionalUnit is intentionally modeled as distinct from both:

Course, which represents a broader curricular aggregation unit; and

EducationalResource, which represents a pedagogical artefact used to support instructional processes.

This distinction allows OntoKSD to separate:

curricular aggregation, represented by Course;

instructional units, represented by InstructionalUnit; and

pedagogical artefacts, represented by EducationalResource.

An InstructionalUnit may therefore:

be associated with one or more competencies through competence-related relations;

occur within an AcquisitionContext;

use one or more EducationalResource instances.

At the current scope of OntoKSD, the minimal descriptive structure of InstructionalUnit includes properties such as title and description, while contextual, competency-related, and resource-related aspects are represented through explicit relations.

Ontological rationale:
InstructionalUnit is introduced to represent pedagogically meaningful units of instruction, activity, or evaluation without conflating them with curricular aggregators or educational artefacts. This modeling choice supports a clear instructional layer in which competence activation is anchored at the level of bounded pedagogical units.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Knowledge
ontoksd:Knowledge rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ;
                  rdfs:comment """A theoretical or practical understanding of facts, concepts, principles, and procedures relevant to a specific domain.

In OntoKSD, Knowledge represents the cognitive dimension of a competence — what an individual knows and can recall, explain, or apply.

It may include both declarative knowledge (factual information) and procedural knowledge (knowing how to perform tasks), and is typically acquired through study, instruction, or experience."""@en ;
                  rdfs:label "Knowledge"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeSkill
ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ;
                       rdfs:comment """KnowledgeSkill
A relator that represents the structured association between a Knowledge element and the cognitive level required for its competent application within a competency specification.

In OntoKSD, KnowledgeSkill is used to model the Knowledge–Skill pair adopted in competency-oriented curriculum models such as CC2020. Rather than treating the pair as a simple informal combination, this class reifies the association between a knowledge component and the cognitive level at which that knowledge is expected to be mobilized, applied, analyzed, evaluated, or created in context.

The core semantic role of KnowledgeSkill is therefore to connect:

a Knowledge element, representing the conceptual content involved; and

a BloomLevel, representing the required cognitive process level associated with that knowledge in the competency specification.

In addition to this core association, a KnowledgeSkill instance may also be annotated with one or more Bloom action verbs used during the Competency Specification Process (CSP) to justify the choice of the assigned Bloom level. These verbs are not constitutive of the relator itself; rather, they function as traceability-oriented justificatory annotations that document the rationale underlying the selected cognitive level.

Modeling KnowledgeSkill as a relator allows OntoKSD to explicitly represent the internal semantic structure of competency specifications, support traceability from CSP decisions, and distinguish the core knowledge component from the cognitive level required for its competent application.

Typical associations:

hasKnowledgeComponent → Knowledge

hasBloomLevel → BloomLevel

optionally, hasBloomLevelJustificationVerb → BloomVerb

Ontological rationale:
KnowledgeSkill is modeled as a relator because it does not merely denote a knowledge element or a skill in isolation, but a qualified association between them. Its identity depends on the linkage between a specific knowledge element and a specific required cognitive level, while justificatory verbs may be attached as supporting annotations for methodological transparency.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeType
ontoksd:KnowledgeType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment """KnowledgeType
A classificatory category used to indicate the type or orientation of a Knowledge element within a competency specification. In OntoKSD, this class is used to distinguish different kinds of knowledge that may contribute to the constitution of a competence, particularly with respect to their role in computing education and practice.

This classification is aligned with the competency-oriented perspective of CC2020, which distinguishes knowledge that is specifically related to Computing from knowledge that is foundational, professional, or associated with an application domain. Accordingly, KnowledgeType supports the semantic organization of knowledge elements according to their educational and professional orientation.

Instances of KnowledgeType should be used as controlled values associated with Knowledge through a property such as hasKnowledgeType.

Suggested instances
computingSpecific

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element is specific to the Computing domain. It includes concepts, principles, methods, models, techniques, and bodies of knowledge that are characteristically associated with Computing as a discipline or profession.

Examples include knowledge related to algorithms, data structures, programming, software design, computer systems, networks, databases, artificial intelligence, and other core areas of Computing.

foundational

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element provides foundational support for learning, reasoning, or practice. Foundational knowledge is not exclusive to Computing, but it is essential for understanding, applying, or advancing computing-related competencies.

Examples may include mathematical reasoning, logic, communication foundations, scientific literacy, and other forms of knowledge that support learning and performance in computing.

professional

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element is associated with professional practice, workplace conduct, or the organizational and social dimensions of computing work. This includes knowledge that supports responsible, effective, and context-aware participation in professional environments.

Examples may include knowledge related to teamwork, communication practices, ethics, workplace norms, project practices, accountability, and professional expectations.

applicationDomain

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element belongs to the domain in which computing is applied. This type captures knowledge of the social, organizational, commercial, scientific, or sector-specific context that informs the design, deployment, or use of computing artefacts and solutions.

Examples may include knowledge related to business, health, education, transportation, public administration, finance, or other domains in which computing competencies are exercised.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Learner
ontoksd:Learner rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> ,
                                ontoksd:Person ,
                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:participatesIn ;
                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation
                                ] ,
                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProfile ;
                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:Profile
                                ] ;
                rdfs:comment """A LearningRole is an educational role assumed by a learner within a course, discipline, or instructional activity. It contextualizes the learner’s participation by specifying the responsibilities, expected behaviors, and competencies associated with that role.

Core Idea

It represents how a learner is positioned within a specific instructional context (e.g., student in Algorithms, group leader in a project, teaching assistant in a course).

It provides semantic links between roles and competencies, enabling the specification of which competencies are required, expected, or likely to be developed when acting in a given role.

It allows for the contextual interpretation of performance evaluations, since competence evidence is relative to the role played by the learner at the time."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningActivity
ontoksd:LearningActivity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalUnit ;
                         rdfs:comment """LearningActivity
A subclass of InstructionalUnit representing a pedagogically planned unit of learner engagement intended to support the development, practice, or reinforcement of competencies.

In OntoKSD, LearningActivity represents a bounded instructional unit in which learners are expected to engage with content, resources, interactions, or guided actions in order to mobilize relevant competencies. It captures instructional situations primarily oriented toward learning processes rather than toward formal evaluative judgment.

A LearningActivity may involve explanation, exploration, practice, discussion, experimentation, reflection, or guided problem solving. It may also use one or more EducationalResource instances and be associated with one or more competencies within an instructional context.

LearningActivity is broader than LearningTask. While a learning task typically denotes a more concrete, bounded, and executable instructional unit, a learning activity may represent a more general pedagogical arrangement that organizes participation, engagement, and learning practice.

Typical examples of LearningActivity include:

a guided classroom discussion on ethical computing;

a collaborative exploration of automata examples;

a lab activity for practicing recursion;

a seminar-based discussion of software architecture alternatives;

a teacher-guided review session before an assessment.

Ontological rationale:
LearningActivity is modeled as a subclass of InstructionalUnit because it represents a bounded pedagogical unit oriented primarily toward learning. It differs from Assessment, whose primary purpose is evaluative, and from EducationalResource, which functions as a pedagogical artefact rather than as an instructional unit.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningNeed
ontoksd:LearningNeed rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment "A gap between current and desired competence levels requiring educational intervention."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningTask
ontoksd:LearningTask rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:LearningActivity ;
                     rdfs:comment """LearningTask
A subclass of LearningActivity representing a concrete, bounded, and executable instructional unit in which learners are expected to perform a specific task in order to develop, practice, or demonstrate competencies.

In OntoKSD, LearningTask is used to model learning situations that are more explicitly goal-directed and operational than general learning activities. A learning task typically requires the learner to carry out a defined piece of work, solve a problem, produce an artefact, analyze a case, or complete a structured assignment within an instructional context.

As a subclass of LearningActivity, LearningTask inherits the general role of supporting competency-oriented learning. However, it is distinguished by its greater instructional specificity, clearer execution boundaries, and stronger association with concrete learner performance. In many cases, a learning task may provide a relevant basis for observing performance and for supporting later evaluative interpretation, although its primary purpose remains instructional rather than purely evaluative.

Typical examples of LearningTask include:

implementing a deterministic finite automaton for a given language;

solving a set of programming problems involving recursion;

modeling a database schema for a case study;

classifying strings according to a formal grammar;

debugging a piece of source code and explaining the identified errors;

producing a report comparing alternative software design solutions;

completing a worksheet on logical propositions and truth tables.

Ontological rationale:
LearningTask is modeled as a subclass of LearningActivity because it represents a specialized form of learning-oriented instructional unit. While all learning tasks are learning activities, not all learning activities are sufficiently concrete, delimited, or task-oriented to qualify as learning tasks. This distinction allows OntoKSD to represent both broader pedagogical arrangements and more specific executable units within the instructional layer.""" ,
                                  """LearningTask
A subclass of LearningActivity representing a more concrete, bounded, and executable instructional unit in which learners are expected to perform a specific task in order to develop, practice, or demonstrate competencies.

In OntoKSD, LearningTask is used to model learning situations that are more explicitly goal-directed and operational than general learning activities. A learning task typically requires the learner to carry out a defined piece of work, solve a problem, produce an artefact, analyze a case, or complete a structured assignment within an instructional context.

As a subclass of LearningActivity, LearningTask inherits the general role of supporting competency-oriented learning. However, it is distinguished by its greater instructional specificity, clearer execution boundaries, and stronger association with concrete learner performance. In many cases, a learning task may also contribute to the production of evidence of competence, although its primary purpose remains instructional rather than purely evaluative.

Typical examples of LearningTask include:

implementing a deterministic finite automaton for a given language;

solving a set of programming problems involving recursion;

modeling a database schema for a case study;

classifying strings according to a formal grammar;

debugging a piece of source code and explaining the identified errors;

producing a report comparing alternative software design solutions;

completing a worksheet on logical propositions and truth tables.

Ontological rationale:
LearningTask is modeled as a subclass of LearningActivity because it represents a specialized form of learning-oriented instructional unit. While all learning tasks are learning activities, not all learning activities are sufficiently concrete, delimited, or task-oriented to qualify as learning tasks. This distinction allows OntoKSD to represent both broader pedagogical arrangements and more specific executable units within the instructional layer.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Person
ontoksd:Person rdf:type owl:Class ;
               rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyLevel
ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:comment """Definition

A qualitative category representing the interpreted degree of proficiency associated with a score range within a proficiency schema.

Comment

In OntoKSD, ProficiencyLevel represents the qualitative interpretation assigned to a score when that score falls within a corresponding ScaleInterval defined in a ProficiencySchema. Examples include categories such as Beginner, Basic, Intermediate, and Advanced.

A proficiency level is not itself a numeric value and should not be confused with the score obtained in an assessment. Instead, it expresses the evaluative meaning assigned to a score range. In this sense, ProficiencyLevel provides the categorical layer through which numeric assessment results can be interpreted in terms of competence demonstration or achievement.

A ProficiencyLevel may optionally be ordered, for example through an ordinal position, to support ranking or progression semantics.

Suggested note

Typical examples of ProficiencyLevel include:

Beginner

Basic

Intermediate

Advanced""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyScheme
ontoksd:ProficiencyScheme rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:subClassOf [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                   owl:onProperty ontoksd:definesInterval ;
                                                                   owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                   owl:onClass ontoksd:ScaleInterval
                                                                 ]
                                                                 [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                   owl:onProperty ontoksd:definesLevel ;
                                                                   owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                   owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                                 ]
                                                                 [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                   owl:onProperty ontoksd:usesScale ;
                                                                   owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                   owl:onClass ontoksd:Scale
                                                                 ]
                                                               ) ;
                                            rdf:type owl:Class
                                          ] ;
                          rdfs:comment """Definition

A classification scheme that interprets numeric scores expressed in a scale by mapping scale intervals to proficiency levels.

Comment

In OntoKSD, ProficiencySchema represents the interpretive structure used to classify assessment scores into proficiency levels. A proficiency schema uses a specific Scale, defines one or more ScaleInterval instances within that scale, and associates those intervals with ProficiencyLevel categories.

This class separates the measurement structure from its interpretive logic. While a Scale defines the numeric range in which scores are expressed, a ProficiencySchema defines how those numeric values are pedagogically or evaluatively interpreted. This distinction allows the same scale to be reused in different contexts with different classification criteria.

For example, two institutions may adopt the same 0–10 scale while using different interval boundaries or different proficiency labels. ProficiencySchema captures this interpretive layer explicitly.

Suggested note

A ProficiencySchema typically:

uses exactly one scale;

defines one or more scale intervals;

defines one or more proficiency levels;

supports the assignment of proficiency levels based on score ranges.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Profile
ontoksd:Profile rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:isProfileOf ;
                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:Person
                                ] ;
                rdfs:comment """A representation of an individual's competences, learning, education, and roles.

Representa o perfil educacional do aluno: competências evidenciadas, papéis, evidências, lacunas, etc.
É a entidade central da visão educacional — é a partir dela que se derivam análises como “lacuna de competência”, “progresso”, “evidências” etc."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#RoleDetail
ontoksd:RoleDetail rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Scale
ontoksd:Scale rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasInterval ;
                                owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onClass ontoksd:ScaleInterval
                              ] ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:maxValue ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:minValue ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:step ;
                                owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ;
              rdfs:comment """Definition

A numeric measurement structure used to express assessment scores within a defined range of values.

Comment

In OntoKSD, Scale represents the numeric framework within which an assessment score is expressed, such as a scale from 0 to 10 or from 0 to 100. A scale defines the valid numeric space for scoring and may be subdivided into one or more intervals for interpretive purposes. Scale does not itself determine proficiency levels; rather, it provides the measurement structure used by a ProficiencySchema to classify scores into proficiency levels.

A Scale is therefore a measurement artifact, not a competence element and not a proficiency classification by itself. Different proficiency schemas may use the same scale while interpreting its score ranges differently.


Typical examples of Scale include:

a score scale from 0 to 10;

a percentage scale from 0 to 100;

any bounded numeric scoring range adopted in an assessment system.""" ;
              rdfs:label "Scale"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ScaleInterval
ontoksd:ScaleInterval rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:intervalDefinedInScheme ;
                                                               owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyScheme
                                                             ]
                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:correspondsToLevel ;
                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                             ]
                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:isIntervalOf ;
                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Scale
                                                             ]
                                                           ) ;
                                        rdf:type owl:Class
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:includesLower ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:includesUpper ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:lowerBound ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:upperBound ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                                      ] ;
                      rdfs:comment """Definition

A bounded numeric subrange of a scale used to classify assessment scores for interpretive purposes.

Comment

In OntoKSD, ScaleInterval represents a specific numeric interval within a Scale, defined by lower and upper bounds and, where necessary, by whether each bound is inclusive or exclusive. A scale interval is used to support the interpretation of numeric scores by associating a score range with a specific ProficiencyLevel.

For example, in a 0–10 scale, the interval [0.0, 3.0] may correspond to a beginner proficiency level, while the interval (8.0, 10.0] may correspond to an advanced level. A ScaleInterval therefore functions as the mediating structure between a numeric score and its qualitative interpretation.

Each ScaleInterval belongs to exactly one Scale and may be defined within a specific ProficiencySchema.

Suggested note

Typical examples of ScaleInterval include:

[0.0, 3.0]

(3.0, 6.0]

(6.0, 8.0]

(8.0, 10.0]""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Skill
ontoksd:Skill rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkillLevel ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onClass <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel>
                              ] ;
              rdfs:comment """The ability to apply knowledge effectively to perform tasks, solve problems, or produce desired outcomes in a given context.

In OntoKSD, Skill captures the functional and operational dimension of competence, encompassing cognitive skills (e.g., reasoning, problem-solving), technical skills (e.g., programming, instrument operation), and interpersonal skills (e.g., teamwork, negotiation).

Skills are observable through performance and are often assessed via demonstrations, simulations, or task completion."""@en ;
              rdfs:label "Skill"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#SkillDimension
ontoksd:SkillDimension rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:comment """SkillDimension
A classificatory category used to indicate the primary dimension under which a Skill element is characterized within a competency specification. In OntoKSD, this class supports the semantic organization of skills according to the kind of capability they predominantly express in educational and professional contexts.

This modeling choice is compatible with the competency-oriented perspective of CC2020, in which skills are treated as the “know-how” dimension of competence and are associated with higher-order cognitive activity, practical performance, and broader forms of capability relevant to competent action. Rather than assuming that all skills belong exclusively to a single cognitive dimension, OntoKSD uses SkillDimension to make explicit the predominant dimension under which a skill is interpreted.

Instances of SkillDimension should be used as controlled values associated with Skill through a property such as hasSkillDimension.

Instance: cognitiveSkillDimension

A skill dimension indicating that a skill is primarily expressed through reasoning, analysis, interpretation, abstraction, evaluation, problem solving, or other forms of higher-order cognitive performance.

This dimension is strongly compatible with CC2020’s treatment of skills as the “know-how” dimension and with its association of skills with higher-order cognitive activity.

Instance: technicalSkillDimension

A skill dimension indicating that a skill is primarily expressed through practical, procedural, or technique-oriented performance in the execution of tasks, methods, tools, or operations.

This dimension is appropriate for skills related to implementation, construction, configuration, debugging, deployment, or other practical forms of computing performance. It is consistent with competency-oriented literature that treats skills as capabilities manifested in practice.

Instance: interpersonalSkillDimension

A skill dimension indicating that a skill is primarily expressed through interaction with other people in collaborative, communicative, organizational, or socially situated contexts.

This dimension is compatible with the broader competency perspective reflected in CC2020 and related curriculum work, which includes interpersonal capabilities as relevant to competent performance.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#StructureType
ontoksd:StructureType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment """StructureType
A classificatory category used to indicate how a Competence is structurally organized. It distinguishes whether a competence is represented as an indivisible unit or as a composition of component competencies.

StructureType is intended to support the structural classification of competencies according to their internal organization. In OntoKSD, this classification is used to differentiate competencies that are modeled as atomic from those that are modeled as composite.

Instances of StructureType should be used as controlled values associated with Competence through a property such as hasStructureType.

Instance: atomic

atomic
A structure classification indicating that a competence is modeled as an atomic unit, that is, as a competence that is not structurally decomposed into component competencies within the ontology.

An atomic competence may still be associated with knowledge, skills, dispositions, or other qualifiers, but it is not represented as being composed of subordinate competencies.

Instance: composite

composite
A structure classification indicating that a competence is modeled as a composite unit, that is, as a competence structurally composed of two or more component competencies.

A composite competence is represented through explicit composition relations that connect it to its subordinate or constituent competencies.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd/rcd#RCD
rcd:RCD rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept
skos:Concept rdf:type owl:Class .


